 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Probably not just you, but Perth's price rises have been smaller than the rest of the country's.**\n\n- **Perth electricity prices** were up **2.4%** in August 2026 compared with August 2025 (ABS CPI). That's the latest figure available.\n- **Australia-wide electricity prices** were up **13.2%** over the same year to August 2026.\n\nThe Perth numbers have been very volatile over the past two years, with swings of several hundred percent in single months. That pattern most likely reflects WA government energy rebates starting and ending, which change the price the ABS records. Perth's price index has been roughly flat since February 2026, so a sudden jump in your bill probably isn't a city-wide tariff rise.\n\nIf your bill still feels high, these are the more likely causes:\n\n- **Usage:** Heavier air-conditioner use in summer, or a hot spell, can double a bill.\n- **Billing period:** Some bills cover more days than usual, or include a catch-up estimate or a final meter reading.\n- **Rebate or concession:** Check whether a WA rebate or concession was applied last quarter and has now stopped.\n- **Tariff or plan:** Your retailer may have moved you to a different plan when your fixed-term contract ended.\n\nTo check, compare your daily usage in kWh (not the dollar total) with the same period last year. Your retailer or the Energy and Water Ombudsman WA can help if the usage looks normal but the charge doesn't.",
